 person in this Iranian  notion of culture has evolved and then  brings this with music at the end of it  which is a excellent treatment actually  a very good take on the book so in  effect what I'm saying is that my if I  want to be a little bit less poetic my  take off this is that yes Iran is going  through a very troubled period in past  40 years I was going through a period of  crisis and if we look back actually as  this colleague said there's a plenty of  one crisis after another but I don't  think that purely political crisis is or  other very negative aspects are going to  basically define this this politically  defines the history of this country well  today it more than anything else  ecology is important Iran is suffering  from a very severe water shortage  because of the climatic climatic changes  and they are beginning to realize that  the real killer is not the United States  is not what they are facing in terms of  the all kinds of strategic issues is the  water which they don't have as much so  these are the new challenges that tend  to shape future Iran and for that matter  to all of the region as environmental  historians will tell you environment  doesn't recognize boundaries so it's the  whole of the region  let's say from Satish all the way to the  eastern Mediterranean that it's facing  the whole of North Africa it's facing  the same problem I have enjoyed  listening to you talk about your book  thank you so much for being here today  with us thank you I'm gradually occasion  for more information about professor  ammonite and his research please visit  our website at macmillan report yale.edu  be sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  thank you so much  you
 

